## Requirements

- Python **3.10**
- A single GPU with ≤16 GB VRAM is enough (Colab/Kaggle free tier works).
- All dependencies with pinned versions are in [`requirements.txt`](requirements.txt):
  torch 2.2.2, torchvision 0.17.2, numpy, Pillow, PyYAML, scikit-image,
  matplotlib, tqdm, lpips, pytorch-fid.

> On Colab/Kaggle a CUDA torch build is already installed — install the rest with
> `pip install -r requirements.txt` (you can drop the torch/torchvision lines
> there to keep the platform's build).

## Environment Setup

```bash
# clone
git clone <your-repo-url> sar2eo && cd sar2eo

# option A: venv
python3.10 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -r requirements.txt

# option B: conda
conda create -n sar2eo python=3.10 -y && conda activate sar2eo
pip install -r requirements.txt

# sanity check (no dataset needed) — verifies the model + infer pipeline run
python tests/smoke_test.py
```

## Dataset Structure

**Dataset used:** Kaggle *Sentinel-1 & 2 Image Pairs (segregated by terrain)*
(`requiemonk`) — Sentinel-1 SAR (VV) + Sentinel-2 RGB, already 256×256, organised
by terrain. Chosen because it is the smallest practical option, runs on the free
tier, and its terrain split makes a leakage-free validation set natural. *(The
prep script also supports SEN1-2 via `--layout sen12`.)*

After downloading + unzipping, build the split:

```bash
python scripts/prepare_dataset.py \
    --src /path/to/sentinel12_segregated_by_terrain/v_2 \
    --dst data/sen12 \
    --val_frac 0.1 \
    --max_per_terrain 4000        # optional cap for free-tier compute
```

This produces the layout `train.py` expects (SAR matched to EO **by filename**),
and splits by **scene** (not random patches) so near-duplicate adjacent patches
never straddle train/val:

```
data/sen12/
├── train/
│   ├── sar/   # single-channel, 8-bit, dB-scaled, min-max to [0,255]
│   └── eo/    # RGB, 8-bit
└── val/
    ├── sar/
    └── eo/
```

## Training

```bash
python train.py --config config.yaml
```

Per epoch the trainer logs train **and** validation loss, writes the raw values
to `outputs/<experiment_name>/losses.csv`, and re-saves the loss curve to
`outputs/<experiment_name>/loss_curve.png`. For this adversarial model it logs
the generator (total / GAN / L1) and discriminator losses. It keeps `best.pth`
(lowest validation L1), periodic snapshots, and `last.pth`.

**Ablation** (one switch in `config.yaml`):

```bash
# baseline: L1 + adversarial
python train.py --config config.yaml
# ablation: L1 only  (set train.use_gan: false, e.g. experiment_name: pix2pix_l1only)
python train.py --config config.yaml
```

## Inference

Conforms to the assignment I/O contract:

```bash
python infer.py \
    --input_dir  <dir of 256x256 single-channel SAR PNGs in [0,255]> \
    --output_dir <dir for generated 256x256 RGB PNGs> \
    --weights    outputs/pix2pix_sen12_baseline/best.pth
```

Output PNGs keep the same filenames as the inputs. Runs on ≤16 GB VRAM (CPU
fallback if no GPU) and needs **no internet** — the architecture is rebuilt from
config stored inside the checkpoint. Verify against the provided format-sample
pack before submitting.

## Evaluation

```bash
# 1) generate predictions for your val/test SAR
python infer.py --input_dir data/sen12/val/sar \
                --output_dir outputs/pred_val \
                --weights outputs/pix2pix_sen12_baseline/best.pth
# 2) compute metrics against ground truth
python eval.py --pred_dir outputs/pred_val --gt_dir data/sen12/val/eo
```

Reports **LPIPS ↓, FID ↓** (primary, perceptual) and **SSIM ↑, PSNR ↑**
(secondary, pixel-level); writes `metrics.json`.

Qualitative triplets (SAR → generated → ground truth) for the report:

```bash
python scripts/make_qualitative.py \
    --weights outputs/pix2pix_sen12_baseline/best.pth \
    --data_root data/sen12 --split val --out outputs/qualitative.png --n 6
```

